A Nautilus developer has stored confidential data on the jump host within Stratos DC. 
To ensure security and compliance, this data must be transferred to one of the app servers. 
Given developers lack direct access to these servers, the system admin team has been enlisted for assistance.

Copy /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg file from jump server to App Server 2 placing it in the directory /home/nfsdata.

SOLUTION
Steps to Complete the Task
1. Verify the file exists on the Jump Host
bash
ls -l /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg
2. Check if /home/nfsdata exists on App Server 2
bash
ssh username@app-server-2 "ls -ld /home/nfsdata"
If the directory doesn't exist, create it first:

bash
ssh username@app-server-2 "sudo mkdir -p /home/nfsdata"
Replace username@app-server-2 with the actual user and hostname/IP for App Server 2 (e.g., banner@stapp02 or tony@stapp02).

3. Copy the file using scp
From the jump host, run:

bash
scp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg username@app-server-2:/home/nfsdata/
Example with typical Nautilus naming:

bash
scp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg banner@stapp02:/home/nfsdata/
If the destination directory requires root privileges, copy to /tmp first and then move it:

bash
# Step 1: Copy to a writable location
scp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg banner@stapp02:/tmp/

# Step 2: Move it to the final destination with sudo
ssh banner@stapp02 "sudo mv /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg /home/nfsdata/"
4. Verify the file was transferred
bash
ssh username@app-server-2 "ls -l /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg"
You should see the file listed in /home/nfsdata/.

✅ One-Liner Solution
If you have permissions to write directly to /home/nfsdata:

bash
scp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg username@app-server-2:/home/nfsdata/
If you need sudo on the destination:

bash
scp /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg username@app-server-2:/tmp/ && \
ssh username@app-server-2 "sudo mv /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg /home/nfsdata/"
📝 Alternative Approaches
Using rsync (preserves attributes, good for large files)
bash
rsync -avz /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg username@app-server-2:/home/nfsdata/
Using sftp
bash
sftp username@app-server-2
# Then at the sftp prompt:
put /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg /home/nfsdata/
exit
Using ssh + cat (if scp is restricted)
bash
cat /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg | ssh username@app-server-2 "sudo tee /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg > /dev/null"
⚠️ Important Considerations
SSH keys vs. passwords
If passwordless SSH isn't set up, you'll be prompted for the password of the destination user. The Nautilus lab environments typically have SSH keys configured between the jump host and app servers, so scp should work without a password prompt.

File permissions
After copying, verify that the file has appropriate permissions:

bash
ssh username@app-server-2 "sudo chmod 600 /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg"
Since this is confidential data, restricting permissions to owner-only (600) is a good security practice.

Verify file integrity
Compare checksums on both servers to ensure the transfer was successful:

bash
# On jump host
md5sum /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg

# On App Server 2
ssh username@app-server-2 "md5sum /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg"
Both should return the same hash value.

🔍 Final Verification Checklist
bash
# 1. Confirm file exists on jump host
ls -l /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg

# 2. Confirm file exists on App Server 2
ssh username@app-server-2 "ls -l /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg"

# 3. (Optional) Compare checksums for integrity
md5sum /tmp/nautilus.txt.gpg
ssh username@app-server-2 "md5sum /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg"
The task is complete once /home/nfsdata/nautilus.txt.gpg exists on App Server 2 with the correct content.

